Underwater backscatter is an emerging technology for low-cost and low-power communication and sensing in underwater environments. However, past underwater backscatter systems have been largely limited to simple on-off modulation schemes, which inherently limit their robustness, adaptivity, and spectral efficiency. This paper introduces AMB, a novel approach that brings advanced modulations to underwater backscatter. Inspired by the design of DC motors, AMB can synthesize any modulation waveform via PDM (pulse density modulation) synthesis. Its design maintains the simplicity and elegance of the backscatter hardware architecture, while exploiting the inherent filtering properties of its piezoelectric transducers. We built a proof-of-concept prototype of AMB and demonstrated its ability to support previously unattainable modulations, including CSS, M-ary QAM, and OFDM. Our evaluation in a river shows that AMB can improve synchronization success by 2 × , increase throughput by 6 × while maintaining over 3 × higher energy efficiency per bit, and support 4 × more concurrent transmissions over state-of-the-art past underwater backscatter systems. These results demonstrate that AMB can bring significant value in improving the robustness, throughput, and efficiency of underwater backscatter.